### DeepProve fits as a proof leaf, not the whole proof

DeepProve now proves full transformer inference end-to-end; the public repo confirms GPT-2, Gemma 3 and Llama 2 support, including token embeddings through next-token output.  Lagrange's August 2026 technical write-up says it can certify a whole generated sequence rather than merely a layer or token. ([Lagrange][1])

So a WorkerRun could contain:

```text
MODEL_CALL #83

provider: local
model_digest: sha256:ABC...
input_digest: sha256:123...
output_digest: sha256:456...

proof:
    type: DEEPPROVE
    proof_digest: sha256:789...
    verifier: deepprove-v...
```

Now you have cryptographic evidence that:

> model M on committed input X really produced Y.

That's substantially stronger than a TEE for that individual computation.

But it **doesn't prove**:

```text
browser search happened correctly
API returned what WorkerKit claims
marketplace submission occurred
tool execution was legitimate
cost was really charged
bounty was really won
```

Those remain separate attestations.

Lagrange themselves demonstrate this composition pattern: their DeepProve integration stores independently verifiable proofs for individual AI decisions inside a larger autonomous application.

